{"reason": "The execution confirms the initial plan's diagnosis: DeBERTa best checkpoint (epoch4, val loss 0.4062) but evaluation yields 0.9019, causing low weight (0.45) and high ensemble loss (0.2733). Historical experiments show mean pooling consistently improves XGBoost over CLS tokens. Thus, two changes are needed: fix DeBERTa evaluation mismatch (training_evaluation) and switch to mean pooling for embeddings (model_design). Data processing remains unchanged.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Change the embedding extraction in extract_embeddings to use mean pooling of last hidden states (hidden_states[-1].mean(dim=1)) instead of CLS token (hidden_states[-1][:,0,:]). This aggregates information from the entire sequence, providing richer features to XGBoost. Historical experiments show mean pooling reduces XGBoost validation log loss from ~0.30 to ~0.27. Implement by modifying the extract_embeddings function; preserve the function signature and return shape (samples, hidden_size).", "training_evaluation": "Fix the inconsistency between the best checkpoint and its evaluation. After loading best_deberta_model.pt, ensure evaluate_deberta is called with the same model and data as during validation. The current code calls evaluate_deberta right after loading, but the high loss (0.9019) suggests a state-dict loading issue or data mismatch. Implement by explicitly verifying model parameters after load (e.g., compare with saved epoch 4 metrics) and re-running validation. Also, skip evaluation during final extraction if already done. This will restore DeBERTa's validation loss to ~0.406, allowing ensemble weight optimization to assign higher weight (~0.6-0.7) and improve ensemble log loss to ~0.22-0.24."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution confirms the initial plan's diagnosis: DeBERTa best checkpoint (epoch4, val loss 0.4062) but evaluation yields 0.9019, causing low weight (0.45) and high ensemble loss (0.2733). Historical experiments show mean pooling consistently improves XGBoost over CLS tokens. Thus, two changes are needed: fix DeBERTa evaluation mismatch (training_evaluation) and switch to mean pooling for embeddings (model_design). Data processing remains unchanged.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Change the embedding extraction in extract_embeddings to use mean pooling of last hidden states (hidden_states[-1].mean(dim=1)) instead of CLS token (hidden_states[-1][:,0,:]). This aggregates information from the entire sequence, providing richer features to XGBoost. Historical experiments show mean pooling reduces XGBoost validation log loss from ~0.30 to ~0.27. Implement by modifying the extract_embeddings function; preserve the function signature and return shape (samples, hidden_size).",
    "training_evaluation": "Fix the inconsistency between the best checkpoint and its evaluation. After loading best_deberta_model.pt, ensure evaluate_deberta is called with the same model and data as during validation. The current code calls evaluate_deberta right after loading, but the high loss (0.9019) suggests a state-dict loading issue or data mismatch. Implement by explicitly verifying model parameters after load (e.g., compare with saved epoch 4 metrics) and re-running validation. Also, skip evaluation during final extraction if already done. This will restore DeBERTa's validation loss to ~0.406, allowing ensemble weight optimization to assign higher weight (~0.6-0.7) and improve ensemble log loss to ~0.22-0.24."
