{"reason": "The execution results show overfitting: DeBERTa best at epoch 3 (val loss 0.3227) then degrades, and eventually val loss jumps to 0.9019 due to using the overfitted model for embeddings. The initial plan's two changes (curriculum learning, attention pooling) directly address these issues by preventing overfitting through longer sequences first and better token aggregation. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-stage training (fine-tuning then early stopping) and attention pooling to improve generalization. These modifications are best applied in model_design (attention pooling) and training_evaluation (curriculum learning loop) modules.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the standard [CLS] token pooling with attention-weighted pooling over all hidden states. Implement a lightweight attention layer: a single linear layer that takes the last hidden states (batch, seq_len, 1024) and outputs a scalar attention score per token. Apply softmax over sequence dimension to get weights, then compute weighted mean of hidden states. This extracts distributed stylistic features. The new pooling will be added after the transformer backbone and before the classification head. Keep the model class name and forward signature; the forward method will return the pooled representation for embedding extraction and logits for classification.", "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-stage fine-tuning with curriculum learning: first stage train with max_length=768 for 2 epochs, then second stage with max_length=512 for remaining epochs. Adjust tokenizer and dataloader to handle the longer max_length in stage 1. Keep early stopping (patience=5) on validation log loss. After fine-tuning with the best model, extract embeddings using the attention-weighted pooling (override the model's forward to return pooled features). The extraction function should be updated to use the new pooling representation instead of CLS. This two-stage approach provides richer contextual learning and prevents overfitting as seen in the baseline run."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show overfitting: DeBERTa best at epoch 3 (val loss 0.3227) then degrades, and eventually val loss jumps to 0.9019 due to using the overfitted model for embeddings. The initial plan's two changes (curriculum learning, attention pooling) directly address these issues by preventing overfitting through longer sequences first and better token aggregation. Similar historical experiments successfully used multi-stage training (fine-tuning then early stopping) and attention pooling to improve generalization. These modifications are best applied in model_design (attention pooling) and training_evaluation (curriculum learning loop) modules.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the standard [CLS] token pooling with attention-weighted pooling over all hidden states. Implement a lightweight attention layer: a single linear layer that takes the last hidden states (batch, seq_len, 1024) and outputs a scalar attention score per token. Apply softmax over sequence dimension to get weights, then compute weighted mean of hidden states. This extracts distributed stylistic features. The new pooling will be added after the transformer backbone and before the classification head. Keep the model class name and forward signature; the forward method will return the pooled representation for embedding extraction and logits for classification.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-stage fine-tuning with curriculum learning: first stage train with max_length=768 for 2 epochs, then second stage with max_length=512 for remaining epochs. Adjust tokenizer and dataloader to handle the longer max_length in stage 1. Keep early stopping (patience=5) on validation log loss. After fine-tuning with the best model, extract embeddings using the attention-weighted pooling (override the model's forward to return pooled features). The extraction function should be updated to use the new pooling representation instead of CLS. This two-stage approach provides richer contextual learning and prevents overfitting as seen in the baseline run."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "Execution results confirm severe overfitting: best validation loss at epoch 3 (0.3227) then degradation, and best checkpoint re-evaluation yields 0.9019. The root cause matches the initial plan's analysis: insufficient regularization and class imbalance. Historical experiments show that combining Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) and Weighted Random Sampling successfully stabilized fine-tuning, enabling more epochs and better generalization. Modifying only 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' directly addresses the classifier head overfitting and sampling imbalance without altering the unchanged components like handcrafted features, XGBoost, or ensemble structure. The historical case also failed when using incorrect XGBoost API, so the current code correctly uses early_stopping_rounds in constructor; hence no change to 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' is needed.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the single dropout layer in the classification head with Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD). Subclass the DeBERTa model to override the forward method: after the pooled output, apply K=4 independent dropout masks, compute logits for each via the classifier weight, then average the logits before softmax. This directly targets overfitting in the classifier head without changing the backbone. Historical experiments confirm MSD reduces variance and stabilizes training, allowing more epochs. Interface: preserve output_hidden_states and return_dict behavior. The classifier weight and bias must remain trainable and shared across K passes.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the DataLoader's shuffle=True with a WeightedRandomSampler using class weights inversely proportional to training set frequencies. Compute the class counts from y_train_labels and assign sample weights. Set replacement=True in the sampler to ensure equal representation per epoch. Historical experiments show this balances gradient contributions from minority authors (HPL, MWS) and improves embedding quality. Keep all other training settings unchanged: AdamW lr=2e-5, linear scheduler, label smoothing, gradient clipping, mixed precision. The validation and test loaders must remain unchanged (no sampler)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Execution results confirm severe overfitting: best validation loss at epoch 3 (0.3227) then degradation, and best checkpoint re-evaluation yields 0.9019. The root cause matches the initial plan's analysis: insufficient regularization and class imbalance. Historical experiments show that combining Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) and Weighted Random Sampling successfully stabilized fine-tuning, enabling more epochs and better generalization. Modifying only 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' directly addresses the classifier head overfitting and sampling imbalance without altering the unchanged components like handcrafted features, XGBoost, or ensemble structure. The historical case also failed when using incorrect XGBoost API, so the current code correctly uses early_stopping_rounds in constructor; hence no change to 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' is needed.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the single dropout layer in the classification head with Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD). Subclass the DeBERTa model to override the forward method: after the pooled output, apply K=4 independent dropout masks, compute logits for each via the classifier weight, then average the logits before softmax. This directly targets overfitting in the classifier head without changing the backbone. Historical experiments confirm MSD reduces variance and stabilizes training, allowing more epochs. Interface: preserve output_hidden_states and return_dict behavior. The classifier weight and bias must remain trainable and shared across K passes.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the DataLoader's shuffle=True with a WeightedRandomSampler using class weights inversely proportional to training set frequencies. Compute the class counts from y_train_labels and assign sample weights. Set replacement=True in the sampler to ensure equal representation per epoch. Historical experiments show this balances gradient contributions from minority authors (HPL, MWS) and improves embedding quality. Keep all other training settings unchanged: AdamW lr=2e-5, linear scheduler, label smoothing, gradient clipping, mixed precision. The validation and test loaders must remain unchanged (no sampler)."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the two-stage pipeline with an end-to-end trainable multi-layer attention pooling classifier. Execution results confirm severe overfitting (val loss 0.38 -> 0.85) and XGBoost dominating the ensemble (weight 0.7), validating the need for Change #1 and #2. Similar historical experiments succeeded by combining handcrafted features with DeBERTa fine-tuning and ensemble weight optimization, but the current plan removes those features. To avoid performance regression, the refinement reintroduces key stylometric features from historical successes as auxiliary inputs to the MLP head, while preserving the end-to-end joint training. The model_design module is modified to implement multi-layer pooling and accept auxiliary features; training_evaluation is updated with the freeze-then-discriminative schedule and Focal Loss. data_processing_and_feature_engineering remains largely unchanged but must output the auxiliary features for concatenation.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the DeBERTa-v3-large model with microsoft/deberta-v3-base. Implement a custom classifier head that performs multi-layer attention pooling over hidden states from layers [-1,-2,-3,-4]: for each of the 4 layers, apply learned self-attention (single-head) over the sequence to produce a pooled vector, yielding 4*384=1536-dim representation. Concatenate this with 39 auxiliary features (stylometric 30, readability 4, POS 5) extracted via data_processing_and_feature_engineering (unchanged pipeline). The combined 1575-dim vector is passed through a 2-layer MLP (768-dim hidden, ReLU, LayerNorm, dropout 0.3) to produce 3 logits. Use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=[0.4,0.3,0.3] based on class frequencies) instead of CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing. The tokenizer remains AutoTokenizer for deberta-v3-base.", "training_evaluation": "Implement a 3-cycle discriminative fine-tuning schedule: freeze backbone for first 2 epochs (train only attention pool and MLP head with LR=5e-5), then unfreeze backbone for remaining 8 epochs with layer-wise LR: backbone layers LR=1e-5 (apply weight decay 0.1), head layers LR=5e-5. Use batch size 32 with gradient accumulation steps=2 (effective batch 64). Optimizer: AdamW (eps=1e-8). Scheduler: one-cycle cosine with total=10 epochs, warmup=2 epochs for head-only phase. Early stopping: monitor val log-loss, patience=3, restore best weights. DataLoader num_workers=0 to avoid fork warnings. Disable mixed precision (no autocast/scaler) for stability. The final model is single end-to-end; no separate XGBoost/LR or ensemble. Submission generation remains identical (output probabilities for EAP/HPL/MWS)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the two-stage pipeline with an end-to-end trainable multi-layer attention pooling classifier. Execution results confirm severe overfitting (val loss 0.38 -> 0.85) and XGBoost dominating the ensemble (weight 0.7), validating the need for Change #1 and #2. Similar historical experiments succeeded by combining handcrafted features with DeBERTa fine-tuning and ensemble weight optimization, but the current plan removes those features. To avoid performance regression, the refinement reintroduces key stylometric features from historical successes as auxiliary inputs to the MLP head, while preserving the end-to-end joint training. The model_design module is modified to implement multi-layer pooling and accept auxiliary features; training_evaluation is updated with the freeze-then-discriminative schedule and Focal Loss. data_processing_and_feature_engineering remains largely unchanged but must output the auxiliary features for concatenation.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the DeBERTa-v3-large model with microsoft/deberta-v3-base. Implement a custom classifier head that performs multi-layer attention pooling over hidden states from layers [-1,-2,-3,-4]: for each of the 4 layers, apply learned self-attention (single-head) over the sequence to produce a pooled vector, yielding 4*384=1536-dim representation. Concatenate this with 39 auxiliary features (stylometric 30, readability 4, POS 5) extracted via data_processing_and_feature_engineering (unchanged pipeline). The combined 1575-dim vector is passed through a 2-layer MLP (768-dim hidden, ReLU, LayerNorm, dropout 0.3) to produce 3 logits. Use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=[0.4,0.3,0.3] based on class frequencies) instead of CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing. The tokenizer remains AutoTokenizer for deberta-v3-base.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a 3-cycle discriminative fine-tuning schedule: freeze backbone for first 2 epochs (train only attention pool and MLP head with LR=5e-5), then unfreeze backbone for remaining 8 epochs with layer-wise LR: backbone layers LR=1e-5 (apply weight decay 0.1), head layers LR=5e-5. Use batch size 32 with gradient accumulation steps=2 (effective batch 64). Optimizer: AdamW (eps=1e-8). Scheduler: one-cycle cosine with total=10 epochs, warmup=2 epochs for head-only phase. Early stopping: monitor val log-loss, patience=3, restore best weights. DataLoader num_workers=0 to avoid fork warnings. Disable mixed precision (no autocast/scaler) for stability. The final model is single end-to-end; no separate XGBoost/LR or ensemble. Submission generation remains identical (output probabilities for EAP/HPL/MWS)."
